+# BLOCKED: 002_unique_email.sql cannot be applied to production
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`migrations/002_unique_email.sql` enforces the requested invariant
+(`users.email` NOT NULL, unique case-insensitively). It can be applied to a
+fresh/clean database, but it **cannot be applied to the current production
+data**, so `test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` fails at the
+`db.apply(conn, "002_unique_email.sql")` line.
+
+## Why
+
+The requirement is that no two users share an email ignoring case and that no
+user lacks an email. `fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql` (2026-10-01) already violates
+both halves:
+
+| rows | problem |
+| --- | --- |
+| `1`, `4`, `23` | `ana.silva@example.com`, `Ana.Silva@example.com`, `ana.silva@example.com` — one address, three users. Rows 1 and 23 are identical in email *and* name. |
+| `17` | `email IS NULL` ("Goran Okafor"). |
+
+Both constraints are validated against existing rows at DDL time, so there is
+no way to declare them while those rows are present:
+
+- `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... ON users (lower(email))` fails: the index build
+  finds ids 1/4/23.
+- `NOT NULL` cannot be added in place in SQLite, so the column has to be
+  recreated via a table rebuild; copying row 17 into a `NOT NULL` column fails.
+
+The two conditions are also mutually unsatisfiable in one migration: SQLite
+validates a `NOT NULL` column against every copied row and a unique index
+against every indexed row, so any migration that succeeds on this snapshot is
+by definition not enforcing the requested invariant.
+
+## Constraints on the fix
+
+Per the task, the migration must not modify or delete customer rows; that data
+is Support's to own. Making the migration succeed would therefore require one
+of the following, all rejected here:
+
+- deleting or merging ids 1/4/23, or rewriting their emails (`id+N@...`),
+  inside the migration;
+- backfilling a placeholder / empty / NULL-target email for id 17;
+- creating a partial index (`... WHERE email IS NOT NULL`) or replacing the
+  constraint with `BEFORE INSERT`/`BEFORE UPDATE` triggers. Both apply cleanly
+  to the snapshot, but they leave ids 1/4/23 and 17 in place: production would
+  keep three accounts sharing one address and one account with no address,
+  while the schema claims otherwise. That is a workaround that hides the
+  problem rather than resolving it, and it would make the prod-snapshot test
+  pass for the wrong reason.
+
+## What was done instead
+
+`migrations/002_unique_email.sql` is written to be correct and safe:
+
+- rebuilds `users` with `email TEXT NOT NULL` and ids/names preserved, then
+  adds `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_nocase ON users (lower(email))`;
+- runs inside a single transaction (`BEGIN IMMEDIATE` ... `COMMIT`), so a
+  failure leaves the table exactly as it was rather than half-rebuilt;
+- checks the existing rows first and aborts with
+  `CHECK constraint failed: email_must_be_present_and_unique_by_lower_email`
+  *before* any DDL when the data cannot satisfy the constraint — it never
+  guesses, never repairs and never drops data.
+
+## Status of the tests
+
+- `test_constraint_on_fresh_db` — **passes**. After `db.migrate()` a NULL email
+  and a case-variant duplicate are both rejected with `IntegrityError`.
+- `test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` — **fails by design**, on the guard, for the
+  data reason above. It is not a migration bug; it is the blocked step. The row
+  comparison in that test never runs because the migration refuses to start.
+
+## What unblocks it
+
+Support needs to decide, for the 2026-10-01 data (and anything added since):
+
+1. ids 1, 4, 23 — which single account is canonical, and how the other two are
+   retired (merged, renamed to a distinct address, or deleted);
+2. id 17 — the real email address, or an agreed policy for accounts that have
+   none (e.g. a verified-email-on-signup flow before the constraint lands).
+
+Once the data satisfies the invariant, `002_unique_email.sql` can be applied
+unchanged, with no edit to the migration and no row touched by it.
diff --git a/migrations/002_unique_email.sql b/migrations/002_unique_email.sql
index 91e11a9..8444ce0 100644
--- a/migrations/002_unique_email.sql
+++ b/migrations/002_unique_email.sql
@@ -1,2 +1,50 @@
 -- 002: users.email must be present and unique, ignoring case.
--- TODO
+--
+-- Email is case-insensitive in practice, so uniqueness is enforced on
+-- lower(email) (SQLite's lower() is ASCII-only; addresses are expected to be
+-- ASCII / punycoded) rather than on the raw column.
+--
+-- This migration changes no rows: if existing data cannot satisfy the
+-- constraint it aborts before any DDL. See BLOCKED.md for the current
+-- production snapshot, which cannot satisfy it.
+
+BEGIN IMMEDIATE;
+
+-- Guard: abort up-front, with a legible error, instead of half-rebuilding a
+-- table whose existing rows already violate the constraint.
+CREATE TEMP TABLE email_constraint_guard (
+    ok INTEGER NOT NULL
+        CONSTRAINT email_must_be_present_and_unique_by_lower_email CHECK (ok = 1)
+);
+
+INSERT INTO email_constraint_guard (ok)
+SELECT CASE
+           WHEN NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM users WHERE email IS NULL)
+            AND NOT EXISTS (
+                    SELECT 1 FROM users
+                     WHERE email IS NOT NULL
+                     GROUP BY lower(email)
+                    HAVING COUNT(*) > 1
+                )
+           THEN 1
+           ELSE 0
+       END;
+
+-- SQLite cannot add NOT NULL to an existing column, so rebuild the table.
+CREATE TABLE users_new (
+    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
+    email TEXT NOT NULL,
+    name TEXT
+);
+
+INSERT INTO users_new (id, email, name)
+SELECT id, email, name FROM users;
+
+DROP TABLE users;
+ALTER TABLE users_new RENAME TO users;
+
+CREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_nocase ON users (lower(email));
+
+DROP TABLE email_constraint_guard;
+
+COMMIT;
